  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Trending seasonably cooler this weekend into \nnext week with bulk of precipitation confined near and southeast\nof Lake Erie\n\nFollowing one of the warmest starts to November on record in\ncentral PA, (see climate section) temperatures will trend \nseasonably cooler/lower this weekend into next week. Persistent\ncyclonic flow over the next 5 days or so with embedded \nshortwave disturbances will offer ample opportunities for precip\ndownwind or southeast of Lake Erie while limiting moisture in \nthe lee of (to the east) of the Allegheny Mountains. A more \nsubstantial long wave trough may attempt to carve out over the \neastern U.S. by the end of the week. \n\nA compact and seasonably strong closed upper low pivots to the \nsoutheast from the thumb of lower MI through CPA tonight into \ntomorrow before lifting out of the area by Sunday morning. This \nfeature accompanied by cool air aloft and mean west to NW flow \nwill bring scattered to numerous showers to start the first full\nweekend of November. Extensive cloud cover and a WNW wind \nshould provide a floor for min temps. A few wet snowflakes can't\nbe ruled out at the peak elevations of the NW Allegheny Plateau\nand Laurel Mtn summits, but no accumulation is expected. \nSaturday will be noticeably cooler and feel rather chilly given \nhow warm it has been during the day over the past week or so.",
